<Project>

  <!--
    Deliberately empty, and its presence is the mechanism.

    MSBuild resolves Directory.Build.props by walking up from the project directory and stopping at
    the first one it finds. Without this file, samples/consumer-proof/ConsumerProof.csproj would
    inherit the repository root's Directory.Build.props - Nullable, LangVersion, ImplicitUsings,
    GenerateDocumentationFile, TreatWarningsAsErrors, NoWarn - and the consumer proof would silently
    be a project built with this repository's settings, which is precisely what it must not be: the
    claim it exists to test is "install the package into a project that has nothing of this
    repository and it works".

    This file therefore declares nothing and imports nothing. It is the boundary, not a settings
    carrier. The few settings the project does need (nullable reference types, implicit usings) are
    written out in ConsumerProof.csproj where a consumer would write them, so nothing about this
    project's build is inherited from the library's repository.
  -->

</Project>
